<% content_for :title, "How it works — What's Your View?" %>

<section class="help-page ui-page">
  <div class="container help-container">
    <header class="ui-masthead">
      <p class="ui-kicker">Using the site</p>
      <h1>How it works</h1>
      <p>Explore a question, give your view and use the fact questions to find out what you know—and what might change your mind.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="help-layout">
      <nav class="help-contents ui-panel" aria-label="On this page">
        <strong>On this page</strong>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#start">Start by browsing</a></li>
          <li><a href="#view">Give your view</a></li>
          <li><a href="#facts">Learn from the facts</a></li>
          <li><a href="#results">Understand results</a></li>
          <li><a href="#trust">Check the evidence</a></li>
          <li><a href="#contributing">Suggest a question</a></li>
          <li><a href="#limits">What results mean</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <article class="help-article">
        <section id="start">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Getting started</p>
          <h2>Look around before you take part</h2>
          <p>You can browse every published topic and its current result without an account. Open anything that catches your attention, then read the full proposition beneath the short title—it tells you exactly what you are being asked.</p>
          <p>Participant numbers matter. Treat a result based on only a handful of people as an interesting early signal, not a firm conclusion.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="view">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Taking part</p>
          <h2>Record where you stand now</h2>
          <p>Sign in, choose the response closest to your present view and then try the fact questions. Being unsure or neutral is a perfectly useful answer.</p>
          <p>You can stop at any time, return later and change your view if the evidence—or further thought—moves you. Your individual responses are not shown publicly.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="facts">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Learning</p>
          <h2>Use the questions as a tour of the issue</h2>
          <p>After every answer you see what was correct, why, and where the information came from. Open the source when you want the context behind a number or claim.</p>
          <p>The quiz normally starts with facts that quickly establish the main issues, then brings in qualifications and evidence that may challenge your starting view. A wrong answer simply shows you something worth learning; only your latest answer counts.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="results">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Making sense of the marker</p>
          <h2>See what people think—and what they know</h2>
          <p>The public marker runs from <strong>No</strong> to <strong>Yes</strong>. Opinions backed by more correct answers to that topic's fact questions have more influence on where it sits.</p>
          <p>That does not make a knowledgeable person's opinion automatically right. It lets you compare a simple show of hands with a result that takes relevant factual knowledge into account. Your weight applies only to that topic.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="trust">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Checking the material</p>
          <h2>Do not take a questionable fact on trust</h2>
          <p>Each answer includes a source. If a fact seems inaccurate, outdated, unclear or irrelevant, signed-in participants can report it for review. Moderators can correct it or remove it from current scoring.</p>
          <p>For the detailed scoring rules and editorial approach, see the <%= link_to "methodology page", methodology_path %>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="contributing">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Contributing</p>
          <h2>Suggest something worth asking</h2>
          <p>The <%= link_to "Proposals page", opinion_question_proposals_path %> lets anyone see exactly what a submission involves and draft the complete form. You will be asked to sign in or create an account only when you are ready to send it.</p>
          <p>A strong proposal asks about one clear, genuinely disputed idea that evidence could help people assess. Moderators may refine its wording and will not publish it until its supporting fact questions have been reviewed.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="limits">
          <p class="ui-kicker">Reading with care</p>
          <h2>The result is a prompt for thought, not a final verdict</h2>
          <p>Participants choose to come here, so they may not represent the wider public. Quiz performance shows knowledge of the selected facts, not general expertise, wisdom or moral authority.</p>
          <p>The useful question is not only where the marker ends up. It is whether the evidence sharpened, strengthened or changed your own view—and which facts made the difference.</p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
